# Handoff Rules

## Sending Handoffs
- Prepare the handoff fields (`type`, `to`, `priority`, `task`, and `commit` or `message`) and call the `swarm_handoff` tool with those fields as arguments — no draft file is needed.
- Use only these message types:
  - `git_handoff`
  - `note`
- Do not send `note` handoffs unless the user, role prompt, or constitution
  explicitly directs you to send one.
- When blocked by ambiguity, contradiction, or test/specification conflict,
  ask the operator by calling the `swarm_clarify` tool with your question.
  Do not ask ad hoc in your session; use the Swarm panel in the dsh Web UI
  (via `swarm_clarify`). Do not send a `note` handoff unless one of
  the explicit authorities above directed that note.
- For `git_handoff`, commit first, then call the tool. The `swarm_handoff` tool fills `commit` and `artifacts` from the sender worktree HEAD; do not type a SHA. The tool infers the role from `roles.tsv` and this worktree.
- If you are the master agent on a pack that includes specifier, queue `git_handoff` when the work is ready. Do not ask for approval in your session; the operator reviews via the Attention queue in the Swarm panel of the dsh Web UI.
- Do not add extra git headers (`coverage`, CRAP, evidence). If evidence is needed, the tool writes it.
- For `git_handoff`, provide:

```text
type: git_handoff
to: <role>[,<role>...]
priority: NN
task: <short-stable-task-name>
```

- When your role is an intermediate step in the pack pipeline, always forward
  a `git_handoff` to the next role in the chain after completing the inbound
  task, regardless of what changed. Formatting-only, manifest-only, audit-only,
  generated metadata, and other non-functional churn still require a forward
  down the chain.
- When your role sends the end-of-chain handoff to multiple recipients, those
  recipients merge only. They do not forward that handoff further. Only this
  terminal broadcast is merge-only without re-forwarding. Do not invent
  `git merge`; the `ready_for_next` tool runs `merge_and_process.sh`.
- Preserve the received task name when forwarding work for the same task.
- The master agent uses the existing New Task / board card name as `task:`. Do not invent a name.
- For `note`, provide:

```text
type: note
to: <role>[,<role>...]
priority: NN
message: <one line, max 80 chars>
```

- If the `swarm_handoff` tool reports validation errors, repair the fields and retry the call.
- Do not write long handoff bodies. The tool generates the delivered payload.
- Do not attempt to notify or wake other roles directly; only the `swarm_handoff` tool triggers delivery and wake-up.
- Do not hand-edit, merge, stage, or commit handoff runtime state.

## Receiving Handoffs
- When notified, call the `ready_for_next` tool. The tool infers the role from `roles.tsv` and this worktree.
- The `ready_for_next` tool dispatches to the task or batch behavior configured for
  your role. For `git_handoff` it merges the inbound commit
  (`merge_and_process.sh <sender> <commit>`).
- If it returns `NO_TASK`, stop waiting for work.
- If it returns `TASK: <path>`, treat the returned `PAYLOAD` as the task.
- If it returns `TASK_NAME: <name>`, use that as the stable task name for any
  work you forward from that task.
- If it returns `BATCH: <path>`, treat each returned `BATCH_ITEM` as part of the
  current batch in tool-delivered order.
- Use only the task information returned by the tools.
- If a followup wake-up arrives while already working on a task, ignore it.
- When the task or batch is fully complete, call the `done_with_current` tool.
- `note` handoffs are tasks too; after reading or acting on a note, call
  the `done_with_current` tool before accepting any other handoff.
- If the `done_with_current` tool returns `TASK: <path>`, treat the returned
  `PAYLOAD` as the next task.
- If the `done_with_current` tool returns `BATCH: <path>`, treat each returned
  `BATCH_ITEM` as part of the next batch in tool-delivered order.
- If a done call returns `NO_TASK`, stop waiting for work.
- On restart, call the `ready_for_next` tool and follow its result.
